README.OSK -- GNU sed 1.06 for OS-9/68000, the source of CMDS/sedWHAT THIS IS  The OS-9/68000 port of GNU sed 1.06 ("GNU sed version 1.06 (or so)"),  exactly as it came: the archive sed_1.06.tar.Z in the GNU directory of  Microware's OS-9 archive.  Nothing in this tree has been changed.  It  was already CR-terminated ASCII.HOW IT RELATES TO THE SHIPPED BINARY  CMDS/sed IS sed_1.06.Z from the same directory of the same archive,  byte for byte (md5 53b50c4144eaf3d4de08b3badb5a2202, 38728 bytes).  The binary and this source were published side by side, under the  same name, by the same porter; this is its source.  SRC/eff_sed (EFFO forum disk 15, SOFTWARE/C/SED) is GNU sed 1.02, an  earlier version and a different port.  It is not this binary's source.  The porter's OS-9 changes are the #ifdef OSK blocks in sed.c and  glob.c.  The porter is not named anywhere in the archive.BUILDING IT  The makefile is the porter's own: it compiles into RELS and links  os9lib.l and os9lib2.l, and swaps in a cpp.new for regex.c because  Microware's cpp stops on regex.c's EXTEND_BUFFER macro (line 179,  "source line too long").  With this collection's driver, tools/rebuild, the recipe is    sed|sed_1.06|sed.c regex.c glob.c|CPP2|/dd/LIB/os9lib.l /dd/LIB/blarslib.l|  CPP2 stands in for cpp.new.  os9lib.l gives alloca; blarslib.l gives  vfprintf, which the porter's os9lib2.l supplied.  Nothing else.  That build is NOT byte-identical to CMDS/sed (it is -qm, trap-free,  47872 bytes; the archive binary uses cio).  Measured on 2026-09-24  against CMDS/sed, on 21 scripts (s///g, s///2, #n, ranges, \( \)  back-references, y, a/i/c, h/H/x, N/P/D, =, q, l, !, &, t and  labels, w file, bracket classes, anchors): identical output every  time.  The collection's own sed cases pass on it as well.  The  archive binary still ships; this tree is here so its source does.TERMS  sed.c, line 3: "This program is free software; you can redistribute  it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License  as published by the Free Software Foundation; either version 1, or  (at your option) any later version."  regex.c and glob.c carry the  same notice.  The archive carries no COPYING file; the GNU General  Public License version 1 is on this disk as SRC/m4_0.5/Copying.FILES  sed.c regex.c regex.h glob.c   the program  makefile                       the porter's OS-9 makefile  README                         GNU's own  ctrans, pascal                 a sample sed script (Pascal to C) and                                 its test input, from the archive  RELS                           empty; the makefile's object directory